\title{
    \vspace{-2em}
    \textbf{\Large AXON-RFC-006 Appendix B (v0.4): \texttt{web\_app} as a Resource}\\[0.3em]
    \large Phased delivery, URA v4.1.5 alignment, test matrices,
    and the collapse of the v0.3 state-type detour
}
\author{
    Silan Hu \\ \small National University of Singapore
}
\date{Draft v0.4, \today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
This appendix specifies how an EasyNet agent authors, builds and
serves a full-stack web application without introducing any new
ontological primitive beyond what URA v4.1.5 and the
\texttt{Resource} ent table (URA v2 plan, phase 2) already provide.
A web app is a \texttt{resource/} URA whose \texttt{kind} is
\texttt{"web\_app"}; build, serve and stop are ordinary ability
invocations whose subject is that resource URA; the receipt chain
required by RFC-001 §1.5 is the receipt chain produced by those
invocations through the daemon's existing dispatcher --- not a
parallel chain owned by this RFC. The Hub serves the static
bundle, translates \texttt{POST /api/<verb>} into the same
\texttt{forward\_invoke} path every other federated invocation
takes, and exposes \texttt{actions.*} as a queryable ability
namespace for peer agents on the owner. Delivery is split into
three phases: (1) single-host local serve, (2) federated cross-host
serve, (3) peer-agent action discovery via \texttt{actions.*}.
Each phase carries an explicit test matrix at three layers
(L1 CLI surface, L2 wire protocol, L3 underlying state). v0.4
deliberately removes from v0.3 the new state-type, the bespoke
state machine, the private content-addressed blob store and the
self-rolled receipt format, all of which are subsumed by
existing infrastructure.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents
\vspace{1em}
\hrule
\vspace{1em}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{What changed from v0.3}
\label{sec:v03-diff}

\derived{} v0.3 invented \texttt{state\_type = "easynet.web\_app"}
with its own state machine (\texttt{Created} / \texttt{Building}
/ \texttt{Built} / \texttt{Failed} / \texttt{Deleted}), its own
ent table layout, its own canonical-receipt format, its own
content-addressed \texttt{TreeBlob} store and a four-segment URA
shape \texttt{r/<realm>/agent/<owner>/web\_app/<slug>}. Each of
these duplicated something that has since landed (or been
planned) elsewhere:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l X X}
\toprule
\textbf{v0.3 invented} & \textbf{Already exists in} & \textbf{v0.4 action} \\
\midrule
\texttt{state\_type=easynet.web\_app} & v4.1.5 \texttt{resource/} URA kind, \texttt{Resource} ent (URA v2 plan §Phase 2) & dropped — web\_app is a \texttt{kind="web\_app"} resource row \\
4-segment URA \texttt{agent/<o>/web\_app/<s>} & v4.1.5 §A.URA-7 forbids 4 segments & rewritten as \texttt{resource/web\_app.<owner>.<slug>} (3 segments) \\
\texttt{webapp.*} ability namespace & ability registry (URA v2 plan §Phase 6) accepts any namespace; subject = resource URA is the v4.1.5 way & abilities renamed \texttt{<owner>.web\_app.<verb>}; subject is the resource URA, admission validates per v4.1.5 \texttt{ValidateSubject} \\
Bespoke 5-state state machine & RFC-001 §1.5 ``receipt chain is the history of state transitions'' & dropped — lifecycle is the receipt chain through the existing dispatcher \\
TreeBlob private blob store & filesystem path on owner host (Phase 1) + content-addressed blobs (Phase 2 only when content needs to move across hosts) & Phase 1 reads \texttt{dist/} directly from owner FS; TreeBlob deferred to Phase 2 \\
Independent receipt store + signing & URA v2 plan §Phase 6 receipt schema + receipt mirror queue & receipts written through the existing receipt store; chain semantics are the existing semantics \\
\texttt{principal/human-anon} caller URI & v4.1.5 only knows 6 kinds (user/device/agent/ability/hub/resource) & anon callers are recorded in \texttt{caller\_context} (main spec §3.6); caller is the Hub URA itself \\
\texttt{easynet-pages-author} skill mixing pages and web\_app guidance & two distinct state types, two skills & split into \texttt{easynet-page-author} (RFC-006-A) and \texttt{easynet-webapp-author} (this RFC, Phase 1) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\noindent The result: v0.4 is roughly one third the surface area of
v0.3, with no loss of capability and no new on-the-wire concept.
The ``what we ship'' picture is now the phased plan in
\S\ref{sec:phases}, anchored by test matrices in
\S\ref{sec:test-matrices}.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{URA shape and resource model}
\label{sec:ura}

\subsection{The web\_app URA}

\normsv{} A web app is a \texttt{resource/} URA with three segments:

\begin{lstlisting}
easynet:///r/<realm>/resource/web_app.<owner>.<slug>
                              └── kind ─┘└── id ──┘
\end{lstlisting}

For the production realm:

\begin{lstlisting}
easynet:///r/easynet.run/resource/web_app.alice.shop
\end{lstlisting}

This is the canonical identity of the web app. It is the value
written to:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item \texttt{Resource.uri} (Unique index in the ent table)
    \item Receipts as \texttt{subject\_uri} for every ability
    invocation that mutates or reads the web app
    \item The Hub's URL-to-resource resolver (path segment maps
    to this URA)
    \item \texttt{caller\_context.target\_resource} when an
    invocation is on behalf of this web app
\end{itemize}

\begin{invariant}[WB-INV-1 (URA shape)]
A web app's URA \textbf{MUST} be exactly three segments,
\texttt{r/<realm>/resource/web\_app.<owner>.<slug>}. The URA
\textbf{MUST} parse cleanly through \texttt{parse\_ura()} (URA v2
plan §Phase 7) and yield \texttt{kind = Resource}. Anything else
is non-conformant.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{Resource row}

\normsv{} A web app's persistent state lives in the
\texttt{Resource} ent row introduced by URA v2 plan §Phase 2. No
new table is added by this RFC.

\begin{lstlisting}
Resource {
  id              = "web_app.alice.shop"
  kind            = "web_app"
  realm           = "easynet.run"
  owner_user_id   = <uuid of alice>
  uri             = "easynet:///r/easynet.run/resource/web_app.alice.shop"
  meta            = {
                      // web_app-specific projection lives in meta JSON.
                      // Schema is owner-controlled; the daemon validates
                      // against the schema below before write.
                      "manifest": {
                        "name":        "shop",
                        "description": "Tiny ceramic shop.",
                        "visibility":  "public"
                      },
                      "build": {
                        "command":           "npm install && npm run build",
                        "dist_dir":          "dist",
                        "build_timeout_secs": 600
                      },
                      "api_surface":     ["shop.list_items", "shop.checkout"],
                      "actions_surface": ["actions.list_for_page",
                                          "actions.get",
                                          "actions.search"],
                      "routes":          [ /* routes_manifest entries */ ],
                      "csp":             { /* csp section */ },

                      // Build artifact: filesystem path on owner host
                      // for Phase 1; content-addressed TreeBlob hash
                      // for Phase 2.
                      "build_artifact_local_path":  "~/.easynet/web-apps/shop/dist",
                      "build_artifact_hash":        null,    // Phase 2

                      // Last-build summary; the receipt chain holds
                      // the full history.
                      "last_build_status":          "ok" | "failed" | null,
                      "last_build_receipt_id":      "r_...",
                      "last_build_at":              "2026-04-29T07:00:00Z",

                      // Runtime overlay (does not enter canonical
                      // hash; cleared on daemon restart).
                      "runtime": {
                        "state":      "Stopped" | "Running" | "Crashed",
                        "bind":       "127.0.0.1:8787",
                        "started_at": null
                      }
                    }
  created_at      = ...
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{notebox}
The choice to inline web\_app fields under \texttt{meta} JSON
rather than introduce a sub-table is deliberate: it lets the
ontology stay closed (one resource table, kind-discriminated)
while letting each kind evolve its meta schema independently.
The meta schema is stored as JSON Schema in
\texttt{src/runtime/agents/web\_app/meta\_schema.json}; the
daemon validates on every \texttt{resource.create} and
\texttt{resource.update}. If the meta schema drifts in the
future, kind-versioning lives inside the JSON
(\texttt{meta.\$schema\_version}).
\end{notebox}

\subsection{State, expressed as receipts not as enum}

\normsv{} v0.3's five canonical state values
(\texttt{Created}, \texttt{Building}, \texttt{Built},
\texttt{Failed}, \texttt{Deleted}) are removed. In their place,
the resource's lifecycle is expressed by the receipt chain:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l X}
\toprule
\textbf{Question} & \textbf{Answered by} \\
\midrule
Does the web app exist? & The \texttt{Resource} row exists. \\
Has it ever built successfully? & A receipt for \texttt{<owner>.web\_app.build} with \texttt{outcome=success} exists in the receipt store, indexed by \texttt{subject\_uri = <web\_app URA>}. \\
What is the last build artifact? & \texttt{meta.build\_artifact\_local\_path} (Phase 1) or \texttt{meta.build\_artifact\_hash} (Phase 2); \texttt{meta.last\_build\_receipt\_id} for the receipt. \\
Is it currently being served? & \texttt{meta.runtime.state == "Running"} and the daemon is listening on \texttt{meta.runtime.bind}. \\
Why did the last build fail? & The \texttt{outcome=failure} receipt's \texttt{state\_transition.reason\_hash} dereferences to the failure reason. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\begin{invariant}[WB-INV-2 (no parallel state machine)]
Implementations \textbf{MUST NOT} maintain a separate state-value
enum or parallel state-transition log for \texttt{web\_app}. The
lifecycle of a web app is the sequence of receipts whose
\texttt{subject\_uri} is the web app's URA, ordered by
\texttt{prev\_receipt\_hash}, walked through the existing
receipt-store \texttt{WalkChain} API.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{Abilities and admission}

\normsv{} A web app exposes its lifecycle through ability
invocations on the owner agent. The ability namespace follows
v4.1.5 ability URI shape \texttt{ability/<owner>.<name>}:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l X}
\toprule
\textbf{Ability URA} & \textbf{Effect} \\
\midrule
\texttt{ability/<owner>.web\_app.create}  & Mint the \texttt{Resource} row + create project dir under \texttt{\textasciitilde/.easynet/web-apps/<slug>/}. \\
\texttt{ability/<owner>.web\_app.update\_config} & Replace declared meta config (manifest / build / api / actions / routes / csp). \\
\texttt{ability/<owner>.web\_app.build}  & Run the declared build command in the project dir. Long-running. Emits start/progress/terminal receipts. \\
\texttt{ability/<owner>.web\_app.serve}  & Start the in-daemon Hub (Phase 1) or register with the federated Hub (Phase 2) so HTTP requests route here. \\
\texttt{ability/<owner>.web\_app.stop}   & Reverse \texttt{serve}. \\
\texttt{ability/<owner>.web\_app.delete} & Implicit \texttt{stop} + remove project dir + delete \texttt{Resource} row. \\
\texttt{ability/<owner>.web\_app.get}    & Return the resource row + a runtime snapshot. \\
\texttt{ability/<owner>.web\_app.list}   & Return the caller's web apps. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

For every invocation:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item \texttt{caller\_uri} = the agent's URA (or the Hub URA
    when the request originated from a browser; see
    \S\ref{sec:hub-caller}).
    \item \texttt{callee\_uri} = the owner agent URA.
    \item \texttt{subject\_uri} = the web app's resource URA.
    \texttt{ValidateSubject} (URA v2 plan §Phase 5) accepts this
    because \texttt{kind = Resource} is in the subject white-list.
    \item \texttt{ability\_uri} = the ability URA above.
\end{itemize}

\begin{invariant}[WB-INV-3 (subject discipline)]
For every ability invocation pertaining to a web app, the
\texttt{subject\_uri} \textbf{MUST} be the web app's resource URA.
Invocations whose subject is the owner's agent URA, the owner's
device URA, or any other non-resource kind are non-conformant
even if the ability happens to operate on the web app's data.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{actions.* abilities are also resource-subjected}

\normsv{} The peer-discovery namespace from v0.3 §6 survives, but
its abilities follow the same admission rule:

\begin{lstlisting}
ability/<owner>.web_app.actions.list_for_page
ability/<owner>.web_app.actions.get
ability/<owner>.web_app.actions.search
ability/<owner>.web_app.actions.list_all
\end{lstlisting}

Subject is always the web app's resource URA; the page path or
intent string is an argument, not a tail. Compared to v0.3's
\texttt{<owner>.actions.<verb>}, the namespace is now scoped
under \texttt{web\_app} so an owner with multiple web apps can
host distinct actions per app without collision.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Hub routing model}
\label{sec:hub}

\subsection{Caller URA at the HTTP boundary}
\label{sec:hub-caller}

\normsv{} When the Hub receives an HTTP request from a browser,
the resulting envelope is built as follows:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item \texttt{caller\_uri} = the Hub's own URA    \texttt{easynet:///r/<realm>/hub} (singleton).
    \item \texttt{caller\_context} carries the request-side
    metadata that v0.3 had wanted to encode in the URA itself
    --- IP-hash, ephemeral session id, user agent. Per main
    spec §3.6 this is exactly \texttt{caller\_context}'s purpose.
    \item \texttt{subject\_uri} = the web app's resource URA
    (resolved from the request path).
    \item \texttt{callee\_uri} = the owner agent URA.
\end{itemize}

\begin{invariant}[WB-INV-4 (no \texttt{principal/} URA)]
Implementations \textbf{MUST NOT} mint URAs of the form
\texttt{principal/...} for HTTP callers. v4.1.5 has no
\texttt{principal} kind. Anonymous request identity belongs in
\texttt{caller\_context}, not in \texttt{caller\_uri}.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{Path layout}
\label{sec:hub-path}

\normsv{} The Hub maps URLs to resource URAs through a single
prefix:

\begin{lstlisting}
GET  /web/<owner>/<slug>/                       → static dist/index.html
GET  /web/<owner>/<slug>/assets/<path>          → static dist/assets/<path>
POST /web/<owner>/<slug>/api/<verb>             → invoke <owner>.<verb>
POST /web/<owner>/<slug>/easynet-for-agents/<v> → invoke <owner>.web_app.actions.<v>
GET  /web/<owner>/<slug>/__easynet/agent        → agent_view JSON
GET  /web/<owner>/<slug>/__easynet/health       → health JSON
GET  /web/<owner>/<slug>/__easynet/receipts     → receipts list (paged)
\end{lstlisting}

The path \texttt{<owner>/<slug>} resolves to the resource URA
\texttt{r/<realm>/resource/web\_app.<owner>.<slug>}. Realm is
inferred from the Hub's bind realm (Phase 1: always
\texttt{easynet.run}; Phase 2: the realm the Hub instance is
serving).

\subsection{Behaviour matrix}

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l l X}
\toprule
\textbf{Resource exists?} & \textbf{Runtime} & \textbf{Hub response} \\
\midrule
No                 & --                & 404. \\
Yes, never built   & --                & 503 \texttt{not built}, \texttt{Retry-After: 60}. \\
Yes, last build failed & --            & 503 \texttt{last build failed} + \texttt{last\_build\_receipt\_id}. \\
Yes, built, Stopped & --              & 503 \texttt{not serving}. \\
Yes, built, Running & --              & full action dispatch (\S\ref{sec:hub-dispatch}). \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

``Built'' here means \texttt{meta.last\_build\_status == "ok"}.

\subsection{Action dispatch}
\label{sec:hub-dispatch}

\paragraph{static} Phase 1: read directly from
\texttt{meta.build\_artifact\_local\_path}. Phase 2: blob
fetch by \texttt{meta.build\_artifact\_hash} from the
content-addressed store the URA v2 plan introduces in §Phase 6.
\paragraph{api:<verb>} Validate \texttt{<verb>} against
\texttt{meta.api\_surface}. Refuse with HTTP 404 (not 403, to
avoid leaking ability existence) when not declared. Otherwise
invoke through the existing path: Phase 1 ---
\texttt{invoke\_local\_ability(<verb>, args)};
Phase 2 --- \texttt{invoke\_via\_federation\_forward}
(\texttt{forward\_invoke}) so the call goes hub-to-owner-to-ability
through the same dispatcher every other federated call uses.
\paragraph{actions:<verb>} Same as \texttt{api}, but verb is
validated against \texttt{meta.actions\_surface} and is invoked
under \texttt{<owner>.web\_app.actions.<verb>}. Default
\texttt{Content-Type} is \texttt{application/json}; the ability
may opt into \texttt{text/eal} when returning EAL mission text.
\paragraph{agent\_view} JSON projection of the resource row
plus a pointer to the receipt-history endpoint. See
\S\ref{sec:agent-view}.
\paragraph{health / receipts} Thin wrappers over the resource
row's runtime overlay and the receipt store query API.

\begin{invariant}[WB-INV-5 (one dispatcher)]
The Hub's API and actions handlers \textbf{MUST} invoke target
abilities through the same dispatch path used by every other
caller (Phase 1: \texttt{invoke\_local\_ability}; Phase 2 and
beyond: \texttt{invoke\_via\_federation\_forward}). They
\textbf{MUST NOT} build a parallel invocation path. This is the
endgame contract Hai-Feng + Liang-Bing converged on for
RFC-002 / federation in the URA v2 plan.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{agent\_view}
\label{sec:agent-view}

\normsv{} \texttt{GET /\_\_easynet/agent} returns:

\begin{lstlisting}
{
  "uri":           "easynet:///r/easynet.run/resource/web_app.alice.shop",
  "kind":          "web_app",
  "owner":         "easynet:///r/easynet.run/agent/alice",
  "manifest":      { ... copied from meta ... },
  "api_surface":   [ "shop.list_items", "shop.checkout" ],
  "actions": {
    "namespace":         "<owner>.web_app.actions",
    "registered_verbs":  [ "list_for_page", "get", "search" ],
    "http_mirror_prefix": "/web/alice/shop/easynet-for-agents/",
    "discovery_hint":
      "POST /web/alice/shop/easynet-for-agents/list_for_page {\"page_path\":...}"
  },
  "build": {
    "last_build_status":     "ok",
    "last_build_receipt_id": "r_a3f2...",
    "last_build_at":         "2026-04-29T07:00:00Z"
  },
  "runtime": {
    "state":      "Running",
    "started_at": "2026-04-29T07:00:12Z"
  },
  "history": {
    "receipts_url": "/web/alice/shop/__easynet/receipts"
  }
}
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent agent\_view is deliberately a projection of the
resource row + a pointer to receipts. It does not embed the
action library (peers query \texttt{actions.*}); it does not
embed canonical state hashes (Phase 2 only when content moves
across hosts).

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Phased delivery}
\label{sec:phases}

The RFC ships in three independently-verifiable phases. Each
phase is gated by its test matrix in \S\ref{sec:test-matrices}.

\subsection{Phase 1 --- Local single-host serve}

\textbf{Scenario.} silan, on his Mac, asks Claude Code to build
a small papers browser. The agent scaffolds a Vite + React
project under \texttt{\textasciitilde/.easynet/web-apps/papers/},
declares two API abilities (\texttt{papers.list},
\texttt{papers.summarize}), builds, and serves on
\texttt{http://127.0.0.1:8787}. silan opens the URL in a
browser and uses the tool.

\textbf{Boundary.} One host, one daemon, one owner, one web app,
no peers, no Hub federation, no \texttt{actions.*}, no receipt
chain walking, no content-addressed blobs.

\textbf{Deliverables.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.12em]
    \item CLI: \texttt{easynet web\_app \{new,build,serve,stop,delete\}}.
    \item Daemon abilities: \texttt{<owner>.web\_app.\{create,update\_config,build,serve,stop,delete,get,list\}}.
    \item In-daemon Axum server bound to \texttt{127.0.0.1:<port>},
    serving \texttt{static} and \texttt{api/<verb>} only.
    \item Project dir at \texttt{\textasciitilde/.easynet/web-apps/<slug>/}.
    \item Skill \texttt{easynet-webapp-author} seeded into each
    agent's workspace at first daemon contact (Phase 1 surface
    only --- no actions, no federation).
    \item Receipts written through the existing \texttt{receipt\_store}.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Out-of-scope (Phase 1).} Cross-host, content-addressed
blobs, \texttt{actions.*}, \texttt{agent\_view}, WebSocket
proxy, peer discovery, public DNS, TLS.

\subsection{Phase 2 --- Federated cross-host serve}

\textbf{Scenario.} alice's web app from Phase 1 is reachable
from bob's machine, both via \texttt{easynet ability invoke}
(\texttt{bob} calls \texttt{<alice>.papers.summarize}) and via
browser at the production Hub
(\texttt{https://easynet.run/web/alice/papers}), with the API
calls flowing \texttt{browser → Hub → forward\_invoke → alice
daemon → ability}.

\textbf{Boundary.} Two hosts, two daemons, federated Hub,
content-addressed dist bundles (TreeBlob arrives here),
receipt chain walked across hosts. Still no
\texttt{actions.*}.

\textbf{Deliverables.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.12em]
    \item Hub at the federated layer (Go backend) gains
    \texttt{/web/<owner>/<slug>/...} routes that resolve to a
    \texttt{resource/web\_app.<owner>.<slug>} URA and
    \texttt{forward\_invoke} into the owner daemon.
    \item Build artifact promotion to TreeBlob in the URA v2
    plan §Phase 6 content-addressed store, with a fallback path
    when the owner daemon is offline.
    \item \texttt{meta.build\_artifact\_hash} populated; the
    Hub serves bytes from the content-addressed store, never
    from owner local FS, in this phase.
    \item Receipt mirror queue (URA v2 plan §Phase 9) carries
    every web\_app receipt to the backend so cross-host
    \texttt{WalkChain} works.
    \item \texttt{agent\_view} endpoint goes live (Phase 1 had
    only the resource row internally).
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Out-of-scope (Phase 2).} Peer-agent action discovery
(\texttt{actions.*}), WebSocket subscriptions, SSR.

\subsection{Phase 3 --- Peer agent action discovery}

\textbf{Scenario.} bob's Claude Code visits alice's
\texttt{/web/alice/papers/cart} URL, calls
\texttt{<alice>.web\_app.actions.list\_for\_page("/cart")},
receives action descriptors, calls
\texttt{actions.get(name="checkout", params={...})}, receives
a fully-literal EAL mission, runs
\texttt{easynet mission run /tmp/checkout.eal --cite-receipt
<actions.get-receipt-id>}, and the resulting receipt chain on
bob's side cites alice's \texttt{actions.get} as
\texttt{causal\_context.Scalar}.

\textbf{Boundary.} Peer agents are first-class consumers; EAL
\texttt{--cite-receipt} flag lands; \texttt{causal\_context} is
populated cross-state-object; agent\_view advertises actions
namespace.

\textbf{Deliverables.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.12em]
    \item \texttt{<owner>.web\_app.actions.\{list\_for\_page,get,search,list\_all\}}
    abilities (owner-implemented; this RFC defines the
    descriptor schema and HTTP mirror, not the body).
    \item \texttt{easynet mission run --cite-receipt <id>} flag
    in EAL CLI; \texttt{causal\_context.Scalar} populated on
    the first step's invocation envelope.
    \item \texttt{agent\_view} \texttt{actions} block populated.
    \item \texttt{easynet-webapp-author} skill updated with
    actions authoring guidance.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Out-of-scope (Phase 3).} Owner-side specialisation
sandbox, signed mission bundles, \texttt{actions.*} versioning.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Test matrices}
\label{sec:test-matrices}

Each phase ships with a three-layer test matrix. Layers follow
the deep-harness convention (L1 CLI / L2 wire / L3 underlying
state). A test case PASSes only when all three layers pass; a
phase ships only when every test case PASSes in CI and in the
docker e2e environment.

\subsection{Phase 1 matrix (13 cases)}

