\section{Migration from v0.3}
\label{sec:migration}

\begin{warningbox}
v0.3 was paper-only (no implementation landed). The following
acts as the cut list for any draft branches or sketches:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item Drop \texttt{state\_type} enum and the 5-state machine.
    Replace with the \texttt{Resource} row + receipt-chain reasoning
    in \S\ref{sec:ura}.
    \item Rename \texttt{webapp.*} ability namespace to
    \texttt{<owner>.web\_app.*}; subject is the resource URA.
    \item Drop the bespoke \texttt{TreeBlob} store from Phase 1;
    serve from local FS. Reintroduce content-addressed storage
    in Phase 2 only.
    \item Drop \texttt{principal/human-anon} URA scheme. Use
    \texttt{caller\_context} on Hub-minted envelopes.
    \item Rename project dir to
    \texttt{\textasciitilde/.easynet/web-apps/<slug>/}.
    \item Split the v0.3 \texttt{easynet-pages-author} skill into
    \texttt{easynet-page-author} (RFC-006-A) and
    \texttt{easynet-webapp-author} (this RFC).
\end{itemize}
\end{warningbox}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Open decisions}
\label{sec:open}

\begin{decisionbox}
\textbf{Phase 1 Hub --- in-daemon Rust axum vs. Go backend.}
v0.4 picks in-daemon axum for Phase 1 because the scenario is
single-host local serve and the daemon already has the ability
dispatcher in-process. Phase 2 promotes the Hub responsibility
to the Go backend so production traffic shares cookie domain,
auth flow, TLS termination, and observability with the rest of
the platform. The handover surface (Hub trait + the
\texttt{forward\_invoke} contract) is the same in both phases;
the change is which binary owns the listener.
\end{decisionbox}

\begin{decisionbox}
\textbf{Build sandboxing.} Phase 1 / Phase 2 build runs in a
subprocess inheriting daemon privileges (with restricted
\texttt{PATH} and minimal env). Full sandboxing
(\texttt{seccomp} / \texttt{pledge} / Docker) is deferred to
RFC-006-B Phase 4, alongside the SSR persistent-process model.
\end{decisionbox}

\begin{decisionbox}
\textbf{Cookie / session for HTTP callers.} Phase 1 and 2 use
ephemeral server-side session ids (cleared on Hub restart) and
record them in \texttt{caller\_context}. Persistent
authenticated humans land via a separate
\texttt{user/<authenticated>} URA scheme outside the scope of
this RFC.
\end{decisionbox}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Coverage check}

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.18}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l X l}
\toprule
\textbf{Rule} & \textbf{Discharged by} & \textbf{Where} \\
\midrule
URA v4.1.5 §A.URA-7 (3-segment URA)  & WB-INV-1 + path \(\to\) URA mapping             & \S\ref{sec:ura} \\
RFC-001 §1.5 (subject = resource)    & WB-INV-3 + admission \texttt{ValidateSubject}   & \S\ref{sec:ura} \\
URA v2 plan §Phase 2 (Resource ent)  & no new table; \texttt{meta} JSON schema         & \S\ref{sec:ura} \\
URA v2 plan §Phase 5 (admission)     & Hub envelope routes through ValidateSubject     & \S\ref{sec:hub-caller} \\
URA v2 plan §Phase 6 (receipt store) & receipts written through existing store          & \S\ref{sec:ura} \\
URA v2 plan §Phase 9 (receipt mirror)& Phase 2 cross-host chain                         & Phase 2 deliverables \\
RFC-002 keyring                      & owner signs receipts via existing keyring path  & \S\ref{sec:ura} \\
forward\_invoke endgame contract     & WB-INV-5 (one dispatcher)                        & \S\ref{sec:hub-dispatch} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\paragraph{Closing.} A web app is a resource. Its lifecycle is
its receipt chain. Its dispatcher is the dispatcher every other
caller uses. Its identity is a three-segment URA. v0.4 keeps
the engineering work that v0.3 framed correctly --- the build
pipeline, the Hub routing, the actions namespace --- and drops
the conceptual surface it duplicated. The phased plan and the
test matrices in \S\ref{sec:test-matrices} are the contract.

\vfill
\hrule
\vspace{0.4em}
\noindent\small
\textit{The agent-native web is not a new ontology; it is the
ontology v4.1.5 already gave us, applied to a long-running
resource. Pages, web apps, and any future authored artifact
share the same shape: a URA, a resource row, a receipt chain,
a dispatcher.}

\end{document}
